{
	title: 'Terminal frames cost what changed, not the history',
	spec: "A terminal frame's cost follows what changed since the last one, not the length of the history: frame.build reuses the transcript rows of the leading items that are the same objects as last frame (same width, session, glimpse size and theme), and lays out only the items after the first change; a streaming item, a question being answered and all after them are laid out every frame. A full redraw (Ctrl-L, resize, another tab) forgets the kept rows with the streaming peaks. A tool result's glimpse lays out only the source lines it shows (each wrapped, and cut to what a glimpse can show) and counts the rest, so a megabyte output costs what three rows cost. The reused frame is identical to one built from nothing. Measured on the user's imported data (task 3q, the 11 MB focused tab of 2,127 items): the first frame of that tab went from 500 ms to 80 ms, a key's frame from 7 ms to 0.2 ms, and the longest stall while typing during startup from 500 ms to under 90 ms. Laying out items is sliced (task 7j): render.draw gives frame.layout a deadline of render.sliceMs() (4 ms); layout stops at the first stable item once it is past, after at least one, keeps what it laid out in frame.state.history and returns nothing, and later slices, each a task of its own (setTimeout 0), go on from there until all is laid out, then paint once. Meanwhile every frame (keys, prompt, chrome, pending prompts) still paints, over the tab's item rows painted last; a tab not painted since the last full redraw (a switch, Ctrl-L, resize) shows only its last screenful of items (render.tail, laid out without keeping) until then. The frame painted at the end is identical to one built at once.",
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Found with a probe typing into ./run on the imported home (/tmp probe script, not committed): frame.build ran itemRows over all 2,127 items on every key (a WeakMap lookup and a key string each, 7 ms), and the first layout of the focused tab spent 350 ms wrapping megabyte tool outputs whole to show three rows. Key echo p50 is about 21 ms with an empty home too, so the remaining latency is not the history.',
		'Reuse is by object identity of items: transcript folding makes a new item for any change, so an edited item is a new object (task e3 notes rely on the same).'
	],
	needs: ['e3', 'te']
